WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.290
The first texture optimization I want to talk about

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.450
has to deal with using streaming virtual textures

3
00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.890
as an alternative way to stream textures for your project

4
00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.529
in from disk.

5
00:00:13.529 --> 00:00:15.449
Now, the reason I bring this up

6
00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:17.429
is traditionally Unreal Engine

7
00:00:17.429 --> 00:00:19.649
has used MIP-based streaming.

8
00:00:19.649 --> 00:00:21.890
With this streaming-based method,

9
00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.609
an entire object actually needs

10
00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:27.250
to be rendered even if a single pixel of that object

11
00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.250
is visible.

12
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.769
So let's take a mountain range

13
00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:31.010
that's off in the distance.

14
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.689
We have this mountain range that's really massive,

15
00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:36.490
and the user can just see a single pixel

16
00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:37.890
of that mountain range.

17
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.890
We have to render that entire mountain range mesh

18
00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.090
even if we can just see that one pixel.

19
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.969
And this can be inefficient, especially as our scenes

20
00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:48.570
grow in size and complexity.

21
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.810
With streaming virtual texturing,

22
00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:53.909
we can actually just see that one pixel

23
00:00:53.909 --> 00:00:55.409
and not have to worry about loading

24
00:00:55.409 --> 00:00:57.289
all of that additional data.

25
00:00:57.289 --> 00:01:00.130
Now, again, this is the default option

26
00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:03.210
for any new Unreal Engine 5 project.

27
00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.450
But if you do not have it enabled

28
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.730
or you enabled it after the fact,

29
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.530
you can enable virtual texturing

30
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.329
by first going up to Rendering

31
00:01:11.329 --> 00:01:14.370
and then enable virtual texturing support.

32
00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:18.829
Once you've done this, you will need to restart the editor.

33
00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:21.750
Once the editor has restarted, depending

34
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.209
on what you have done previously,

35
00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:27.030
you'll need to do one of two things.

36
00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.430
First, you'll need to either convert any existing textures

37
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:34.670
to utilize virtual textures by selecting all those textures,

38
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.549
right-clicking on them inside of the content browser,

39
00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:40.969
and then converting them to virtual texture.

40
00:01:40.969 --> 00:01:44.069
Unreal will automatically convert all textures

41
00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:45.950
to use virtual textures.

42
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.189
It's a simple check mark box.

43
00:01:47.189 --> 00:01:49.750
And again, this can be done for you automatically

44
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.670
by selecting all the textures you want

45
00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.870
in the content browser, right-clicking on them,

46
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:54.790
and then from the menu,

47
00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.109
selecting Convert to Virtual Texture.

48
00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.629
When you utilize a texture or want to know

49
00:02:00.629 --> 00:02:03.829
if a texture you're about to use is a virtual texture,

50
00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:05.750
it will have a VT logo

51
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.909
on the lower right-hand corner of the texture,

52
00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:11.189
as we can see on the slide in front of us.

53
00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:12.789
Once we're using that virtual texture

54
00:02:12.789 --> 00:02:14.389
inside of a material,

55
00:02:14.389 --> 00:02:16.330
we'll need to make sure that the sampler type

56
00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:19.270
is set to Virtual Color, Grayscale,

57
00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.750
Alpha, Normal, Masked, Linear Color,

58
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.770
Linear Color, Grayscale.

59
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.250
The reason I'm bringing this up

60
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:29.810
is Unreal Engine 5 will automatically apply this setting

61
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.889
to any new texture samplers that utilize virtual textures,

62
00:02:33.889 --> 00:02:35.530
but existing materials

63
00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.930
may have to have their samplers converted.

64
00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.129
So just make sure that the converted option

65
00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:43.830
matches the original input in that texture sampler.
